I have no detailed knowledge on all of these, but I will try some rough
analysis nonetheless, just knowing the probability of 5,7% for
generation of healing potions.

An important figure that I don't know is the average number of potions
generated on each level.  From my playing experience, I will take this
to be about four.  I presume, it is smaller in general, but the four
accounts for liquor emporia as well.  I will then assume that 25 dungeon
levels have been explored, so one hundred potions should have been found.

Next, I do not think that the probability that *no* potion of healing
will be generated is the important question, although it is easy to
calculate.  It will be p^100=0.0028, i.e. 0.28 %, with p = 1-0.057.  So
the case that Janis described, i.e. NO scroll of enchant armor (similar
probability) in 27 levels of dungeons, is very rare indeed, but it will
be encountered by frequent players -- about 3 times in 1000 games played
up to (or rather down to...) 25 levels.  Which means more often, if many
of the games finish prematurely :-).

If you take the situations, where *any one* of ten items occuring at
similar probability (potion of healing, scroll of enchant armor, scroll
of enchant weapon,...) does not appear up to level 25 (or 17, if you
count in the gnomish mines), then you are at about 3 games in 100, and
many more, if the average occurrence is not four but only two items per
level.

But the interesting question for leveling up to about 300 hit points is
not how frequently there is no potion of healing but how frequently
there are only a few, as you need about 15 to 20 of them to attain this
level of HP.  (And contrary to what Derek asserts, the important figure
is not the probability of generated items but the probability of
generated *and accessible* items; so with the playing style of Janis, he
should get at all of them.  A potion of healing quaffed by an
out-of-sight monster is useless for the player; hence the probabilities
should be adjusted downward.  Nevertheless, with Janis, I believe this
will not change things a lot; with his playing style, similar to mine in
early game, he will certainly not miss more than 20% of the generated
items.)

O.k., so what is the probability of *not* having more than 5 potions of
healing in the first 25 levels if on average four are generated per
level?  This is governed by the binomial distribution.  With p as
defined above, it will be
sum_{k=0}^5 (100 over k) q^k p^(100-k)
where q = 0.057 and p=0.943.

The result of this sum of six terms is 0.44.

So with a probability of 44%, there will be no five healing potions on
the first 25 levels.  If the game happens not to produce liquor shops,
then the average number of potions per level may be two only, in which
case the same figure would be valid for the first 50 levels.  (Actually
I think that this is a bit low. The total number of potions in a full
game is closer to 200 than to 100, IMO.)

Of course, this estimate is only as reliable as the numbers put in it.
Does anyone have better information on the average number of potions (or
scrolls, for that matter) encountered per level?

